# Variable Scope

Every time we declare a variable with `let` or `const`, it gets three things: a name, a value, and a **scope**. The scope determines where in our code that variable can be referenced. Getting it wrong is one of the most common sources of confusing bugs.

Broadly there are two kinds:

- **Global scope**, which can be used anywhere.
- **Local scope**, which can only be used in specific places.

A variable's scope is decided by *where* it is declared, and partly by *how*.

## How Scopes Get Created

JavaScript uses **lexical scoping**, which means a variable's scope is determined by where it sits in the code as written, not by what happens at run time. Three things create scopes:

- The global scope is created automatically.
- Every **function call** creates a new local scope.
- Every **block** creates a new local scope. A block is code between a pair of curly braces: the body of an `if`, a loop, and so on.

There are a few other cases, but those three cover nearly everything.

## Global Scope

Any variable declared outside of any block has global scope, and can be used anywhere. Global variables are even shared **across files**: if `file_1.js` declares `x` at the top level, `file_2.js` can read it. (There is one exception, modules, which we will meet in Course 3.)

## The Scope Stack

Local scopes **stack**. The global scope is at the bottom, and each new local scope is pushed on top of the one containing it.

The rule that matters: **a scope can see variables declared in scopes below it on the stack, but never above it.** So if there are four scopes stacked up, scope 2 can see scope 1 and the global scope, and cannot see scopes 3 or 4.

```{javascript autorun}
const x = 1;

if (x > 0) {
  const y = 2;
  console.log(y);
}

console.log(x);
console.log(y);
```

`y` was declared inside the `if` block, so it belongs to that block's scope. Once we leave the block, that scope is gone, and the last line gets a `ReferenceError`: `y is not defined`. Delete that line and the rest runs cleanly.

Nesting stacks the scopes deeper:

```{javascript autorun}
const x = 1;

if (x > 0) {
  const y = 2;
  console.log(y);

  if (y > 0) {
    const z = 3;
    console.log(z);
    console.log(x);
  }
}
```

Inside the inner `if`, all three variables are reachable. Reading `x` there works like this: the engine looks for `x` in the innermost block, does not find it, looks in the enclosing `if` block, does not find it, looks in the global scope, and finds it. It searches downward through the stack, never upward.

## Every Loop Iteration Gets Its Own Scope

Because a block creates a scope, and a loop body is a block, **each pass through a loop creates a fresh scope**:

```{javascript autorun}
for (const item of [1, 2, 3]) {
  const x = item;
  console.log(x);
}
```

The first pass creates a scope where `item` is `1` and `x` is `1`. That scope is discarded, and the second pass creates a new one where both are `2`. Three passes, three separate scopes, three separate `item` variables.

That sounds like a technicality. It is about to matter a great deal.

## Function Scope and `var`

There is a second kind of local scope. **A variable declared with `var` has function scope**, meaning it belongs to the whole function it was declared in, regardless of which blocks it happens to sit inside.

```{javascript autorun}
function foo() {
  const x = 1;

  if (x > 0) {
    const y = 2;
    var z = 3;
    console.log(x, y, z);
  }

  console.log(z);
  console.log(y);
}

foo();
```

`y` was declared with `const`, so it belongs to the `if` block and is gone after it. `z` was declared with `var`, so it belongs to `foo` and is still readable after the block ends. The last line fails on `y`.

That might even look convenient. It is not, and here is why.

### The Bug `var` Causes

Three fruit buttons and a price tag. We loop over the fruits, and give each button a click handler showing that fruit's price.

```html id=page1
<button id="apple">apple</button>
<button id="banana">banana</button>
<button id="pear">pear</button>
<div id="price-tag"></div>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const prices = { apple: 6, banana: 8, pear: 10 };

for (var id of ['apple', 'banana', 'pear']) {
  const button = document.querySelector('#' + id);

  button.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const priceTag = document.querySelector('#price-tag');
    priceTag.textContent = id + ' costs ' + prices[id];
  });
}
```
```+id=page1
```

Click apple. It says `pear costs 10`. Click banana: `pear costs 10`. Only pear is right.

The reason is scope. `var id` has function scope, so it is **not** recreated on each pass; there is exactly **one** `id` variable shared by all three handlers. The loop finishes long before anyone clicks, and by then that single variable has been left at its final value, `'pear'`. Every handler reads the same variable and sees the same thing.

Change one word:

```{javascript autorun}
const prices = { apple: 6, banana: 8, pear: 10 };

for (const id of ['apple', 'banana', 'pear']) {
  const button = document.querySelector('#' + id);

  button.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const priceTag = document.querySelector('#price-tag');
    priceTag.textContent = id + ' costs ' + prices[id];
  });
}
```
```+id=page1
```

Now each button reports its own price, because `const id` is block-scoped and each iteration has its own.

**One more trap.** If you forget to declare a variable at all and just write `id = ...`, JavaScript quietly declares it for you **with `var`**. So the broken version above is exactly what you get from a missing `const`, with no error to warn you. This is the clearest reason to always declare your variables, and to use `let` and `const` when you do.

## Exercises

1. Declare a variable inside the `if` block and copy its value into `result`, which is declared outside.

    ```{javascript}
    let result = '';

    if (true) {
      // Write your code here
    }

    console.log(result);
    ```
    ```+solution
    let result = '';

    if (true) {
      const inside = 'block value';
      result = inside;
    }

    console.log(result);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(result).should('equal', 'block value').run('Declare a variable inside the block and assign its value to `result`', 'Read an inner variable from the outer scope');
    ```

2. This loop attaches a handler to each button, and every button reports the wrong color. Fix it by changing how the loop variable is declared.

    ```html id=page2
    <button id="red">red</button>
    <button id="green">green</button>
    <button id="blue">blue</button>
    <p id="chosen"></p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const output = document.querySelector('#chosen');

    for (var color of ['red', 'green', 'blue']) {
      const button = document.querySelector('#' + color);

      button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        output.textContent = 'you chose ' + color;
      });
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page2
    ```
    ```+solution
    const output = document.querySelector('#chosen');

    for (const color of ['red', 'green', 'blue']) {
      const button = document.querySelector('#' + color);

      button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        output.textContent = 'you chose ' + color;
      });
    }
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#red').click().run('Could not find the `#red` button', 'Clicked red');
    assert('#chosen').should('have.text', 'you chose red').run('Clicking red should say `you chose red`. `var` gives all three handlers one shared variable; use `const` so each pass has its own', 'Red reports itself');
    assert('#green').click().run('Could not find the `#green` button', 'Clicked green');
    assert('#chosen').should('have.text', 'you chose green').run('Clicking green should say `you chose green`', 'Green reports itself');
    ```

3. Why does the `var` version of the fruit loop show `pear costs 10` for every button?

    ```{mcq}
    With `var id` as the loop variable, every button reports the last fruit. Why?
    correct: Correct! `var` is function-scoped, so all three handlers share a single `id` variable. The loop finishes before any click happens, leaving that one variable at `'pear'`.
    wrong: Revisit "The Bug `var` Causes", and think about how many `id` variables exist in each version.
    [x] `var` is function-scoped, so there is one shared `id`, left at `'pear'` by the time any handler runs
    [ ] The handlers were attached to the wrong buttons
    [ ] `prices` only contains an entry for pear
    [ ] Click handlers always read the last value of any loop
    ```

